A||||4||prof bobo||What size rear axle nut?||||1||
Z||000000||prof bobo||03-26-2001||01:39 PM||||Not the lug nuts, the BIG axle to hub nut.  Looks like somewhere between 28 - 31 mm.  What size socket do I need?  Can it be reused?  (Pontiac shop manual sat to discard it)||||reg||1
Z||000001||mrfiero||03-26-2001||01:41 PM||||It's 30mm......they make sockets specifically for the axle nuts, so I would go buy one (around $10).<P>I have reused them with no problems.  Just inspect it for any flaws (stripped threads, etc.).<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Carrolles||03-26-2001||01:51 PM||||You can also borrow (leave a refundable deposit of around $15) the socket as well as most specialty tools from AutoZone.<P>I've used their axel nut sockets, spring compressors, tie-rod end removal tools, etc.<P>This is a very nice service they offer.<P>Hope this helps,<P>Carroll<P>BTW I agree with the 30mm size.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||03-26-2001||08:33 PM||||The nut is not intended to be reused. You can do it but it's not intended... Torque table for the nut is in my cave. Nut is 30mm.<P>The nut is a couple dollars. You can get them anyplace. I won't reuse one more than 1 or 2 times.<P>Watch it.... that thing gets HOT comming off. Best to remove it and final torque it with the wheel on and the car on the ground. (The socket will fit even with most AL wheels on.) If your parking brake is non working have someone step on the brakes to keep the car still.||||reg||1
Z||000004||c m west||03-26-2001||09:51 PM||||30mm nut on 1.75 pitch - torque to 200 ft lbs||||reg||1
